<article class="env-lemma" id="05Z1">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/05Z1">Lemma <span data-tag="05Z1">66.33.1</span>.</a> Let $X$ be an algebraic space over $\mathbf{Z}$. Let $T$ be an affine scheme. The map </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \mathop{\mathrm{Mor}}\nolimits (X, T) \longrightarrow \mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits (\Gamma (T, \mathcal{O}_ T), \Gamma (X, \mathcal{O}_ X))  \]
</div>
  <p> which maps $f$ to $f^\sharp $ (on global sections) is bijective. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      We construct the inverse of the map. Let $\varphi : \Gamma (T, \mathcal{O}_ T) \to \Gamma (X, \mathcal{O}_ X)$ be a ring map. Choose a presentation $X = U/R$, see Spaces, Definition <a href="/tag/0263" data-tag="0263">65.9.3</a>. By Schemes, Lemma <a href="/tag/01I1" data-tag="01I1">26.6.4</a> the composition 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \Gamma (T, \mathcal{O}_ T) \to \Gamma (X, \mathcal{O}_ X) \to \Gamma (U, \mathcal{O}_ U)  \]
</div>
    <p>
       corresponds to a unique morphism of schemes $g : U \to T$. By the same lemma the two compositions $R \to U \to T$ are equal. Hence we obtain a morphism $f : X = U/R \to T$ such that $U \to X \to T$ equals $g$. By construction the diagram 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix{ \Gamma (U, \mathcal{O}_ U) &  \Gamma (X, \mathcal{O}_ X) \ar[l] \\  &  \Gamma (T, \mathcal{O}_ T) \ar[lu]^{g^\sharp } \ar[u]^{\varphi }_{f^\sharp } }  \]
</div>
    <p>
       commutes. Hence $f^\sharp $ equals $\varphi $ because $U \to X$ is an étale covering and $\mathcal{O}_ X$ is a sheaf on $X_{\acute{e}tale}$. The uniqueness of $f$ follows from the uniqueness of $g$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>